**Item chosen: P8** — “night stages have no headlight beams; add two cheap cone volumes or a spot light plus road decals that stay inside the frame budget” (lowest P number in `docs/QUALITY-QUEUE.md`).

**What the item actually was.** Half of it had already landed and was never recorded: the SpotLight pool and the two additive cone shells exist (`src/render/headlights.js`, added in `099c8c4`) and are pinned by T29/T30. So the run spent itself on the first night frame ever captured — one headless Chrome pass, `?seed=95` (a real DUSK stage, tod 0.85) `&frames=900&shot=1&cam=BUMPER&stats=1`, read back at `shots/quality-night-beams.png`. Judgement of my own shot: the pool does land corridor-sized on the road, but **the shafts rendered as two solid pale cards welded to the nose** — the ramp opened at `a: 0.34` *at the lens*, and every driver camera sits at lamp height *inside* the cone, so the near wall of both shells filled the lower half of the frame as geometry, the far half (where the pool actually lights the road) was the dim part of the curve, and the doubled core (`2 × 1.0 × 0.16` = 0.32 additive) was brighter than the road it drew over.

